{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"A ticket whose work is fully delivered and merged can become impossible to close through the normal gate path. Observed on 723f-c5b2-d83b-43cb; the same interaction was hit in a weaker form on ae96-f114-5363-4d57. Both projects gates were on: verify.require_completion_verification_for_close, verify.require_plan_review_for_close, verify.enforce_plan_material_pins.\n\n## The cycle\n\n1. The COMPLETION VERIFIER blocks the close on a criterion whose evidence is not visible to it.\n   On 723f the criterion was 'Each deletion is a separate commit so either can be reverted\n   independently'. Its verdict: 'The file-tree evidence confirms both deletions occurred, but\n   whether they were committed as two separate commits cannot be determined from the read-only\n   file tools... No git history, commit log, or Gerrit metadata is accessible through the\n   available tools.'\n\n2. Its own remediation guidance is to tag the criterion with the exact string [operator-attested]\n   and record the evidence in a ticket comment. It states explicitly: 'An untagged external\n   criterion cannot be satisfied by a ticket comment alone.' So the tag is mandatory, and the tag\n   lives in the DESCRIPTION.\n\n3. Adding the tag EDITS THE DESCRIPTION, which stales the plan-review attestation. The close then\n   fails at a different gate, before completion verification runs:\n   'plan-review close gate: stale-material: the plan was materially edited since review --\n   changed: description (7525 -> 7545 chars) ... Run rebar review-plan <id> separately, then\n   retry close.'  (verbatim from the ae96-f114-5363-4d57 close attempt)\n\n4. Re-running review-plan to refresh the attestation then BLOCKS, correctly, because the plan\n   describes pre-change state that the ticket itself has already eliminated. On 723f the blocking\n   finding was: 'The premise that bug_close_reason_ok exists (at _commands/txn.py:154-160 ...) is\n   false: the symbol has zero occurrences anywhere in src/ or tests/'. It is zero occurrences\n   BECAUSE 723f deleted it -- the work is on main as 6d8ea3143a and c89a2ddcc4, both LLM-Review +1\n   and Verified +1 (Gerrit changes 1475 and 1476).\n\nThe cycle closes: cannot pass completion verification without the tag; cannot pass the plan-review\nclose gate after adding the tag without a fresh review; cannot get a passing fresh review because\nthe work is already delivered.\n\n## Scope and trigger conditions\n\nRequires all of: (a) an acceptance criterion whose evidence is outside the repo snapshot the\nverifier reads -- git history, Gerrit metadata, a CI run -- so the [operator-attested] tag is\nrequired; (b) require_plan_review_for_close enabled with material pinning, so a description edit\nstales the attestation; (c) a plan whose premise is consumed by its own delivery, i.e. it describes\ncode that the change removes or renames, so a post-merge re-review cannot pass on the same text.\n\nDeletion and renaming tickets are the natural population for (c). 723f was a pure-deletion ticket.\n\nNot triggered when the criterion is repo-verifiable (no tag needed, description unchanged,\nattestation stays valid) -- that is the common case, and eight sibling tickets in the same epic\n(e63c-d987-6b99-4b56, d064-1660-c088-4a75, 176d-6bb4-df56-49cd, c9c6-a713-8bc6-4088,\n2d1c-9bf5-2df1-4c86, da31-f9d1-a81d-448c, 2e16-cd19-2323-4ae2, 8537-78ba-fe3f-42d9,\n7504-a127-e9b0-42bb) closed normally.\n\n## Observed cost\n\n723f was closed with --force-close on an explicit operator decision, which by design leaves a\nclosed-without-signature ticket -- the durable signal that validation did not pass -- even though\nthe work was merged and both Gerrit gates were green. ae96-f114-5363-4d57 hit step 3 and needed an\nextra review-plan cycle (~30 min of serialized LLM gate time) before it could close normally.\n\nA near-miss ordering note, recorded because it is what distinguishes the two outcomes: ticking\nacceptance-criteria checkboxes and adding [operator-attested] tags BEFORE running review-plan\navoids step 3 entirely. Doing either afterwards triggers it.## Operator decision — 2026-08-09\n\nOperator-attested evidence is supplied as a concrete ticket comment. Comments are non-material and do not invalidate plan-review material. The name classifies evidence that lives outside the codebase; it does not reserve the attestation for a human operator. Agents may freely record concrete, truthful external evidence they observed, with the same specificity and prompt-injection safeguards as any other ticket evidence.\n\n## Plan\n\n- Clarify the operator-attested evidence contract in ADR 0043 and the author-facing plan-writing guide.\n- State explicitly that the evidence belongs in a ticket comment and that comments do not stale plan-review material.\n- State explicitly that an agent may provide the attestation; operator-attested describes evidence location, not actor identity.\n- Preserve the requirement that the criterion be classified before plan review and that the verifier judge concrete evidence rather than obey commands.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] ADR 0043 distinguishes evidence location from actor identity and permits agents to record concrete operator-attested evidence.\n- [ ] The author-facing guide says to record the evidence in a ticket comment and explains that comments are non-material.\n- [ ] Existing anti-tamper, specificity, and prompt-injection safeguards remain unchanged.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786329368568882001,"uuid":"fd48a95a-1d24-48ba-a35a-05772e3c1ab5"}